            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for an elementary teacher of letters, then the Latin for a schoolfellow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ēdiscō, ēdiscere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ēdiscō, ēdiscere — \"to learn by heart\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puerī carmina poētae ēdiscunt. The boys learn the poet's poems by heart."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ēdiscō, ēdiscere, ēdidicī: ē + discō, to learn out to the end."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ēdiscō is built from ē \"out, to the end\" and discō \"to learn\" (Lewis & Short): to learn right through."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
