          "title": "You'll want to know: studeō, studēre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "studeō, studēre — \"to be eager (for); to study\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Litterīs studet. He is devoted to literature."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "studeō, studēre, studuī + dative: litterīs studēre, to give oneself to literature. Studium is its noun. English student and study."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "studeō is usually traced to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to push, to strike\" (EtymDB): to press on towards a thing. English student and study come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
